From b2059e323c4cde47532d1fef5ac59f089ae34711 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 14:39:42 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Visible weather under the new look: grey skies, lit rain, wet
 ground and lightning

Elwynn's weather now shows as WoW Forever's does. The server sends 1.12's
own weather; the new look reads the same state and dresses it.

Overcast: the painted sky's cover closes over grey, the sun's light dims
and cools, the god rays and the grade's warmth fade, and the sun shadows
soften to a grey day's. The mist and the wind already followed it.

Rain: 1.12's drops are drawn as streaks lit by the hour's light and the
torches near them, leaning downwind. Rings spread on the water near the
camera. The ground, the cobbles, the stone and the roofs darken in soft
patches over a minute of rain and take a wet sheen: the sun where it
reaches, the sky at a grazing angle, and every torch pooling on the wet
road at night. They dry over about five minutes after.

Storm: heavier rain, gustier wind, and about every half minute a brief
cool double flash that lights the world and blooms, with thunder a few
seconds after if a thunder sound is found in 1.12's sound table. The
strikes run from the weather packet now showing (its arrival, seeded by
the server's day and its grade), so every hero who heard it sees the same
flashes; the server's storm cue keeps them from repeating. Not the game
clock, which Elwynn pins at 23:30 and sets back every five minutes.

worldWeather 0 turns all of it off on its own; lookweather <clear|
overcast|rain|storm> [0-1] holds a weather here for film through 1.12's
own path, and lookweather server lets go (back to the last weather the
server sent). worldLook 0 is 1.12's weather exactly.

Revised after the first weather films (#271, take 272):

- Night rain at Goldshire filmed near-black and murky, darker and flatter
  than the dry frame. By night an overcast takes 0.15 of the moon's light
  (by day 0.5 of the sun's), soaked ground darkens by 0.1 (by day 0.3), and
  the weather lifts the mist to 0.4 of its daytime lift.
- The wet ground mirrors the lights: every new-look light (a lamp, a torch,
  a building's window light) lies on wet stone as a streak from under it
  toward the camera, out to two and a half times its radius, brightest in
  the puddles, where the old sheen was a small highlight inside the light's
  own pool.
- The streaks catch the lamps: 2.0 of a lamp's light, was 0.8, out to 1.6
  times its range.
- The thunder played kit 8432 (ThunderaanAttack, a boss's attack). It is
  chosen now only from kits whose every file sits under the world's ambience
  or weather sounds, never a creature's, a spell's, a character's or an
  item's, nor a name of an attack, wound, death, aggro, cast or impact; the
  pick is logged, and with none the storm is silent.

Revised after the third films (#271, take 273):

- Night rain was no longer near-black but a flat grey murk (90% of the
  pixels between 26 and 46), and as flat with worldWeather 0: it was 1.12's
  storm fog (Elwynn's start of -0.5 of a 278 yd end, a grey veil a third
  thick at the eye) under the night grade. By night the overcast now takes
  it over: the fog starts at least a tenth of its end out (no veil at the
  eye), its end goes 1.8 times further, and the fog's colour keeps 0.45 and
  the sky's 0.6. By day 1.12's storm fog stands.
- No streak showed from the lanterns or the cart lamp. The wet block does
  read the look's light table and its count (a test now checks it, and that
  a lamp 3 yd up lies on the wet road as a streak toward the camera, bright
  on the line between them and nothing 2 yd aside). A lamp with no pool has
  no light in that table, and looklights now says what the table holds: the
  count, and the six nearest lights' distance, height, reach and strength.
- The thunder was silent ("no ambient thunder kit found"). Classic has no
  weather thunder kit at all (its weather kits are the rain, snow and
  sandstorm loops; every kit named for thunder is Thunder Bluff, a thunder
  lizard, Thunderaan or Thunder Clap, per Wowhead's Classic sound database,
  whose ids match 1.12's SoundEntries). The one environmental strike is
  Blasted Lands' ambient lightning bolt, BlastedLandsLightningbolt01Stan,
  kit 4936: a storm's strike now plays it, found by its id or its name, and
  the log says what it resolved to (its files). One strike a flash, 0.3 to
  1.5 s after it (was 1.2 to 6 s, for a distant roll), each a little quieter
  (up to a quarter) or higher or lower (6% either way) than the kit, fixed by
  the strike, so repeats differ. Without it in the data, the storm stays
  silent.
- The flash rises over 0.035 s (two or three frames) to 0.7 of what it was,
  where it was one frame's white-out (a frame mean of 140 to 160 of 255).

Revised after the fourth films (#271, take 280):

- The lamps' reflections never reached the road: the nearest lamps stood
  27 to 41 yd off, and a reflection faded with the ground's distance from
  its light out to 2.5 times its 10 yd pool, so a streak about halfway
  to the camera kept an eighth. It reaches 4 times the pool now, half
  strength at 20 yd. looklights also gives each light's place in WoW
  coordinates, so a film can stand beside a lamp.
- A strike's thunder now says so in client.log as it plays ("weather: a
  strike's thunder plays kit ..."), a storm with no kit says it once, and
  on a machine whose world sound is off each flash says there is no
  thunder, so a film without audio can still be checked.
- The flash jumped in one frame at the rig's 30 fps: it rose over
  0.035 s. It rises over 0.09 s now, three frames at 30 fps, and its
  flicker climbs too and comes later (0.24 s, was 0.17).

Revised after the fifth films (#271, take 291):

- Goldshire's lamp posts made no light: lamppost.m2 #12341 stood 19 yd
  from the camera with no light in the table nearer than 26.9 yd, so no
  streak on the wet road (and no glow in the mist, #273). A prop casts
  light only through a point light in its own model, and the lamp posts
  have none that casts. Under the new look each lamp or lantern prop
  with no light of its own within 2.5 yd now gets a stock torch's light
  half a yard under its top (look::lamps); it goes with its prop and
  with worldLook 0, and each is logged once ("[look lamps]").

